Z||000003||banditbalz||07-08-2001||03:07 PM||||I wanna find myself a early '80s Suzuki Katana. They came without a full fairing and were painted up silver and blue. Very mean looking bike. Had pop up headlight as well. Just like our Fieros, they were ahead of their time...<BR>The Suzuki Bandit 600 as posted below, is very reminiscent of the Katana.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bandit.jpg">  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/banditbalz/" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/banditbalz/</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||14
Z||000004||LarryB||07-08-2001||03:33 PM||||Unless you're a small person and have no interest whatsoever in highway riding stay away from a 250CC, or you'll most likely very soon regret it.   <P>For most people (women included) your first bike should be at least 400CC, and if its under 600CC figure on wanting to upgrade within 2 years.  (I went from 400 to 750 to 900CC in 3 years, back then I weighed about 190 lbs.) New bikes depreciate in value quickly, so IMO your first bike should also be a good used one to lessen the financial bite.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||07-08-2001||03:54 PM||||I measure my bikes in CI not CC... so you can figure American made... and that really narrows it down a lot don't it... hehe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||12
Z||000006||SilverFieroV6||07-08-2001||10:41 PM||||Shaun I would definately go with something small thats not to powerful.  As a new rider you have enough to learn than being distracted by having more Hp than you need.  I have raced street bikes for six years and I bought my first bike for street purposes last July, I bought a 1974 yamaha rd350. The 350 has alot of torque, but not the top speed that new bikes have.  One advantage to a vintage bike is the cornering is no where near as good as a new bike, so when upgradeing to a new bike in a few years you will have better riding skills.  I would say go with a few year old 250  or get a vintage bike you can find them out there all over the place.<P>Hey bandit  I know of a old katana, unfortunately it is race ready  no lights or that type stuff just a tach and bodywork.<BR>Sam<P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)<P>[This message has been edited by SilverFieroV6 (edited 07-08-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by SilverFieroV6 (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||07-08-2001||11:31 PM||||My first bike was a Kawasaki KZ-400. I outgrew it in a big hurry. I would recommend a used 600. Small and inexpensive enough to learn on, big enough that you won't get too bored once you get proficient.<BR>Another advantage that I found with small bikes is that they generally handle better, due to their lighter weight. <BR>I had a Honda VF-1100 Sabre. Was insanely fast in a straight line, but handled like a steamship. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||07-09-2001||04:49 AM||||If you are really just learning to ride, I wouldn't go with a crotch rocket.  I'd lean more towards a generic street bike.  Not a cruiser, either.  Alot of cruisers can be a handful and most crotch rockets can get away from you quickly if you're not paying attention.  Besides, the more generic the bike, the cheaper it is.  There are tons of 500cc-750cc street bikes out there for just a few hundred bucks.  Buy one and ride the heck out of it until you learn.  Buy a crotch rocket next year.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000009||mr_rainmaker||07-09-2001||11:51 AM||||which sportbike you get will depend on your size and ability. how tall are you and how much do you weigh? how much exper do you have? the reason I am asking this is I used to own a bike shop and I am coming across deals on bikes all the time. let me know what you want and I will look around for ya.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>looking to get a sportbike.  Only have about $2000 to spend right now on one.  looking for nothing bigger then a 750 although I would prefer a 600 as I am a beginner.  Don't feel like killing myself too quickly.<P>I am in the daytona beach area so if anyone has any leads on a good one let me know.  I am also willing to take a drive to get a good one too but nothing in cali. Thanks<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||6
Z||000010||Shaun41178||07-09-2001||09:01 PM||||I am 5'11" and 150 pounds.  Yea I am a bit scrawny but can hold my own.  <P>I have no riding experience at all unless you count the years growing up riding a schwinn<P>You can keep an eye out for me Rainmaker but oklahoma is a bit far for me to go for a bike unless it is a super super super super deal that I can't pass up.  Trying to find something more local in the Daytona Beach floida area as I don't have transportation right now.<P>Guess I should let it out now.  The Fiero Gt has been sold.  <P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||07-09-2001||11:35 PM||||FWIW... Riding a motorcycle in the rain really sucks, unless you're really dedicated.<BR>If you're gonna be in Daytona Beach, at least you won't freeze. <P>If this is going to be your only ride, I would highly recommend a windscreen of some type.<P>This was not meant to discourage you, but worth thinking about. <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||mr_rainmaker||07-10-2001||11:12 AM||||well my first sportbike was a cbr1100xx and I  did just fine just don`t be careful at all times. I just bought a gsxr750 that I had shipped across the country for about 400 bucks but I did only pay 1000 for the bike. if I was you I would be looking for a cbr they have a little more room for us in the tall range. I was going to recomend a fzr600 but its kinda of a cramped bike great to start out on though. <BR>||||reg||1
