Z||000014||Philphine||07-11-2001||12:01 PM||||i'd like one now but up to this point i've never had a full street bike. i have an '83 honda xl600 dual purpose bike. just got it out of storage (been there for years) and i'm fooling with it to get it servisable again.<P>if it's just for local riding a big street trail bike is great. real neutral riding position, you sit up pretty high in traffic, the big ones are quick enough to keep you from being a moving roadblock in traffic, and also pretty nimble with a good set of tires. not too bad for a short day trip either. plus dual purpose bikes don't change much stylewise so it dosen't look too dated, if that matters. i'd look for one that came out just when they were switching from kick start to electric start, there are a few with both (mine is just kick, it'll wear you out if it gets touchy).<P>i plan to keep mine forever, but i would like a street bike to compliment it. like an fj1200 yamaha or some other sport tourer type bike.   <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fierokid87||07-11-2001||12:44 PM||||Well  I dont know how to ride.  My budy Kevin is going to let me learn on his Susuki SV 650.  Then when I know how to ride Ill look at one.  My budy Kevin also has a Susuki GTS-R 1000.  That thing flys.  Tops out about 180mph.<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Fierokid's  Web site<BR>http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero<P>AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||87ECO5||07-13-2001||10:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>My first bike was a Kawasaki KZ-400.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ditto.  I kept it for about a year then unloaded it.<P>A few years later, I got back into the bike game when I "stole" (i.e. great price) a 600 Hurricane.  Had it for two months before I crashed it.<P>6 months later, I was back on the road, this time, with a GSXR750.  Kept it for a few months before I crashed it.  That's OK, I didn't really like it anyway.<P>Dropped back down to another 600 a year after that - CBR600F2.  Loved that bike - even though I crashed it!  Man, anyone got some training wheels!?!?!?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>If you're going "crotch rocket", go 600.  Light weight, well-balanced, powerful enough that you won't out-grow it immediately.||||reg||6
Z||000017||EdsB52||07-13-2001||12:40 PM||||Hey 87ECO5, what year was your GSXR? If 93-95, it was a pig. Good power and handling, but a bit heavy. In 96 they dropped over 100lbs! Now they haul ass and are nimble. If you remember in 86 when they came out, they were feather light, but lacked power and flexed in the corners. So Suzuki kept beefing them up and finally went with water cooling in 1993. It seemed Suzuki followed kawasaki instead of maintaining their own identity for lightweight. But in 1996 they regained their identity. I've owned 4 Gixxers and probably would not buy another make.<P>If I were a beginner looking for a sport bike w/2,000 bucks, I would save more and wait for what I want. There's nothing worse than settling for what you can get, instead of buying what you want. There are plenty of kick-ass 600's out there as well as 750's. The old principal of buying a small bike and graduating was started by Japanese bike makers trying to sell more bikes. I could easily teach an inexperienced rider how to jump on a Goldwing or ZX-11 without killing himself. If you buy a do-do bike (anything that's not a roadracer) with the thought of graduating to something you want, you'll be quickly (probably the first week) dissatisfied and lose money in the exchange process. Gixxer 600's seem to suck. The best 600 is probbaly the CBR. The best 750, well you know I like the GSXR, but with 2,000bucks you'll be riding a beat up 93, so that won't work. Ninja's are OK and have a more mild power band, with smoother slightly harder power. If you could grab like 4,000, you would open up your possibilities. Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||mr_rainmaker||07-13-2001||02:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by spideyfoot:<BR><B>Hey Mr Rain Im hunting a Ducati 851 or 888 or 916 that is almost somewhat affordable.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>whoa a duck! depends what you mean  affordable? ||||reg||7
Z||000019||artherd||07-14-2001||03:55 AM||||I must confess  a weakness for the Honda VFR.<P>It's a classy bike in any year, and will likely be quick and powerful enough for any rider.	<P>That said, I want a 748 or 916 like yo-don't-know-what :)  Someday.  (oh, and it'd have to be all on a private track; personally, motorcycles scare the CRAP out of me on public roads, just IMHO!!! :P)<P>Good luck you, and at least take a test ride (but first get a good frid to teach you how to ride, then get a motorcycle license) on a Honda VFR 750. (the new 850 is nice too, but somehow not as charming as the 750.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000020||spideyfoot||07-14-2001||08:57 AM||||Well I figure that there may be a 851 or 888 somewhere in the 5 range out there. I almost picked up a nice 851 for three but the guys had no idea about its history so I didnt know what shape the belts desmos ... might be in. But it was a fun fast bike. The riding position is pretty aggressive but I figure I could get used to that. ||||reg||1
