Z||000022||87ECO5||07-16-2001||10:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Hey 87ECO5, what year was your GSXR?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1987||||reg||1
Z||000023||EdsB52||07-17-2001||12:42 PM||||750 or 1100? Either way, they're about the same. I bought a GSXR 1100 new in July 88. I loved that bike. Since then I bought a used 86 750, 93 750, and 93 1000. The 93 1100 was a piece of crap, except that it was so incredibly fast it was a good drag racer. I think they advertised 0-60 in just over 2 seconds. It was a tank though; I hit a pigeon at 135 indicated and it obliterated the thing with out putting a scratch on the bike! I think a Harley would out-corner the thing. The problem with the first generation GSXR's (86-87 750's; 86-88 1100's) was the frame would flex in the corners and set up a real weird dynamic with sticky tires. And the air/oil-cooled engine was also a problem. The first generation GSXR's made the sport bike world grow up, even though some will say it was the interceptor. The interceptor couldn't hold a candle to any GSXR in regard to handling or speed. I truly believe the GSXR was a milestone motorcycle when it came out.||||reg||1
Z||000024||87ECO5||08-03-2001||09:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>750 or 1100?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>750||||reg||1
Z||000025||LT1 Chop Top||08-03-2001||01:37 PM||||I would have to discourage you from getting a 250 cc also.  I thought a 250cc would be good to learn on, and it was, but after 2 weeks it sucked (Honda Rebel).  It could hardly do 70, and took forever to get there.  I have a Pacific Coast 800cc now, and it is about right.  I would much rather a 1800 Goldwing however!||||reg||1
